  {
   "id": 302,
   "type": "MarkdownNote",
   "pos": [
    0,
    -850
   ],
   "size": [
    1500,
    760
   ],
   "flags": {},
   "order": 5,
   "mode": 0,
   "inputs": [],
   "outputs": [],
   "title": "READ ME",
   "properties": {},
   "widgets_values": [
    "# Method B: a new face reference from the refined face\n\n*The author's method to keep the same face between a normal clip and its FaceRefine Plus version.*\n\n**The problem:**\n- H3 draws a face in a wide shot from very few pixels.\n- FaceRefine Plus re-draws it with detail, guided by the face reference and the keyref.\n- The refined face is sharper, but it can look like a slightly different person from the same character in the clip's close-ups. The close-ups were rendered straight from the original reference.\n\n**The idea:** use the refined face as the character's reference from the start, so every shot is built on the same face.\n\n1. Make the clip from the original face reference (clip workflow).\n2. Run FaceRefine Plus on the wide shot (one-shot workflow, with its keyref).\n3. **This workflow:** take one frame of the refined shot, crop the face, and let Qwen Image 2.1 turn it into a neutral reference portrait. The identity comes from the refined face only. This is `face_reference_B.png`.\n4. Rebuild the character sheet from the new reference, then re-render the clip **from scratch**: same prompt, same seed, new reference and new sheet.\n5. Run FaceRefine Plus on the new wide shot. The refine now pulls toward a face the close-ups already have.\n\n**When:** at the start of a new video, before the other clips. It changes the protagonist's face slightly, so don't switch mid-project.\n\n**Measured** (ArcFace cosine, the clip's close-ups vs the refined wide shot, higher = same person): on the four example characters the refined wide shot went from 0.55 to 0.65 on average (README table). On a music video it went from 0.56 to 0.75."
   ],
   "widgets_values_named": {
    "text": "# Method B: a new face reference from the refined face\n\n*The author's method to keep the same face between a normal clip and its FaceRefine Plus version.*\n\n**The problem:**\n- H3 draws a face in a wide shot from very few pixels.\n- FaceRefine Plus re-draws it with detail, guided by the face reference and the keyref.\n- The refined face is sharper, but it can look like a slightly different person from the same character in the clip's close-ups. The close-ups were rendered straight from the original reference.\n\n**The idea:** use the refined face as the character's reference from the start, so every shot is built on the same face.\n\n1. Make the clip from the original face reference (clip workflow).\n2. Run FaceRefine Plus on the wide shot (one-shot workflow, with its keyref).\n3. **This workflow:** take one frame of the refined shot, crop the face, and let Qwen Image 2.1 turn it into a neutral reference portrait. The identity comes from the refined face only. This is `face_reference_B.png`.\n4. Rebuild the character sheet from the new reference, then re-render the clip **from scratch**: same prompt, same seed, new reference and new sheet.\n5. Run FaceRefine Plus on the new wide shot. The refine now pulls toward a face the close-ups already have.\n\n**When:** at the start of a new video, before the other clips. It changes the protagonist's face slightly, so don't switch mid-project.\n\n**Measured** (ArcFace cosine, the clip's close-ups vs the refined wide shot, higher = same person): on the four example characters the refined wide shot went from 0.55 to 0.65 on average (README table). On a music video it went from 0.56 to 0.75."
   },
   "color": "#432",
   "bgcolor": "#653"
  },
